     "answer": "We deliberately took an ordered, globular member. Methodology says LEA14's defined three-dimensional structure gives a stable scaffold for the protein-protein interactions that its stabilisation function runs on, and that mutation methods written for folded proteins cannot be read the same way on a highly disordered peptide. Unlike BoPep4 it does not depend on one dedicated receptor, so its protection can involve many cellular partners. Its 150-plus residues also pose a different design problem from a short receptor-binding peptide, with far more positions to mutate, and its drought, osmotic and salt protection gives a measurable wet-lab phenotype.",
