     "answer": "Arithmetic settled it with no test run. A hollow-fibre membrane has to run at a wall shear rate of 2,000 to 6,000 s⁻¹, which fixes the cross-flow once the geometry is chosen. The scale-up option, a 63 mm by 300 mm module of about 1,300 fibres and 1.3 m², needs 16.25 to 48.74 L/min; the scale-down option, 10 mm by 600 mm with 11 fibres and 0.02 m², needs 129 to 388 mL/min, which the 1.5 L/min pump reaches. The gap is a factor of ten to thirty, so no experiment was needed. We should also say our own records carry two module geometries, 150 cm² across eight fibres frozen on 4 July and 200 cm² across eleven characterised on 16 August, and the numbers must not be mixed.",
