     "id": "HW-14",
     "type": "alternative",
     "q": "Immersed probes, clamp-on sensors and commercial in-line monitors all exist. Why build your own?",
     "q_zh": "浸入式探頭、夾持式感測器與商用在線監測器都已存在。為什麼要自己做？",
     "answer": "The comparison table on the photometer page gives each one its due. Benchtop cuvette spectrophotometry is the accuracy reference we check against, but every reading removes material from the culture, so it is disruptive, discontinuous and impractical overnight. Immersed probes read continuously but occupy vessel volume, complicate sterilisation and are generally expensive. Clamp-on sensors such as ClampOD are simple and cheap but have their path length fixed by whatever tubing is present, which is precisely the constraint that limits high-density accuracy. Commercial in-line monitors solve the problem well and are priced out of reach for most student teams. Ours gives continuous in-line measurement at a short, chosen path length at student-project cost, around $150 against the $15,000 Bio-Drop, and does not replace a benchtop reference.",
     "answer_zh": "光度計頁的比較表對每一種都有交代。檯面比色槽分光光度計是我們用來對照的準確度基準，但每次讀數都要從培養液取出材料，所以具干擾性、不連續，過夜不可行。浸入式探頭能連續讀取，但佔用槽內體積、使滅菌複雜，且通常昂貴。像 ClampOD 這類夾持式感測器簡單便宜，但光程被現有管徑固定，而這正是限制高密度準確度的瓶頸。商用在線監測器解得很好，但價格超出多數學生隊伍的能力。我們的方案以學生專案的成本（約 150 美元，對比 15,000 美元的 Bio-Drop）提供在短且自選光程下的連續在線量測，且不取代檯面對照儀器。",
     "cite": [
      "hardware/photometer/"
     ],
     "trap": "Claiming novelty for the ratiometric design. The page says splitting a reference off the source is standard photometric practice, and credits ClampOD by Bertaux and colleagues as the closest published design.",
