     "answer": "Primary containment is a PES hollow-fiber cartridge with a 0.2 µm nominal pore, which the Safety page argues is below the 0.5 to 1.0 µm diameter of a B. subtilis cell. Secondary containment is three detectors: transmembrane-pressure leak detection, droplet leak detection and shell-side turbidity for escaped cells. The barrier is only a cell-scale barrier: Results notes a 0.2 µm microfiltration membrane still passes debris and vesicles in the 20 to 200 nm range along with the protein. It also only covers the membrane: Measurement states the tubing, connectors and pump head are part of the containment boundary and no instrument watches them, and version 1 of the reactor ended early when medium leaked at the pump head, which carries lumen-side culture.",
